{"availability":"available","full":"giga-dylan/capy","name":"capy","description":"Desktop app for running coding agents on local LLMs (macOS)","category":"Other","language":"TypeScript","stars":0,"forks":0,"created":"2026-10-01T16:53:18Z","updated":"2026-10-01T19:57:23Z","branch":"main","homepage":null,"topics":[],"demo":null,"color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z","lastDemoCheckedAt":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z","discoveredVia":[],"metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z","stars":0,"forks":0}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Capy is a desktop app for coding agents that run entirely on your Mac. Ask it to change code, explain a project or just chat. A capable agent reads your files, plans, edits and runs commands, and asks before it touches anything. The model, the agent and your data all stay on your machine.","Under the hood Capy bundles two open-source engines and makes them feel like one app:","Capy starts and supervises both, wires them together, and surfaces every opencode setting in a real settings screen, so you never touch a terminal or a JSON file unless you want to. Nothing is sent to a cloud model provider; there isn't one configured."],"features":["opencode is the agent: sessions, tools, permissions, agents,","Ollama runs the model on Apple's MLX engine, the fastest","Local models are smaller than frontier models. Expect good results on focused tasks","and slower, less reliable ones on sprawling changes. Qwen 3.8 27B on an M5 Max generates 30 tokens/s and reads a 16K-token prompt at 930 tokens/s.","Memory decides the model. 27–30B models need about 32 GB; the first load of a model"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/giga-dylan/capy/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"02d1ec1260f9e5c1382d66c0e2d7a6c9710b99df2a4028f1842307bf73ca67d1","extractedAt":"2026-10-03T08:36:20.183Z"},"overviewCheckedAt"